One word, so that qwentts.cpp's ggml compiles for HarmonyOS.

ggml-cpu.c guards its thread-affinity code with __gnu_linux__ in five places
and with __linux__ in one. OHOS is Linux on musl: it defines __linux__ and not
__gnu_linux__, so it takes that one branch alone, and the branch calls
pthread_setaffinity_np and pthread_getaffinity_np. The OHOS sysroot declares
neither, and libc.so exports neither -- only sched_setaffinity. So the build
fails in that branch with eight undeclared-identifier errors, and would fail at
link even if they were silenced.

Making the guard match the other five drops OHOS into the file's own
"unsupported platforms" fallback, where affinity and priority are no-ops. That
is already what llama.cpp's copy of this file does on this target, and thread
affinity is not something this app asks for.

Carried here rather than fixed in the checkout because the checkout is somebody
else's and gets updated. harmony/build.cmd applies it and is idempotent.

--- a/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
+++ b/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
@@ -2634,7 +2634,7 @@
     return true;
 }

-#elif defined(__linux__)
+#elif defined(__gnu_linux__)
 // TODO: this may not work on BSD, to be verified

 static bool ggml_thread_apply_affinity(const bool * mask) {
